title Season G twelfth rung — scalar multiply-add mod L in pure Rye
prompt Take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first on the Rye-first crypto-parity wave; Rye prioritized, Glow on green-witnessed Rye; approve your own design rounds and make progress before panicking to check in; send each finished increment as its own signed round; stop only at custody gates. baton prin recur.
think With Ed25519 VERIFICATION whole and sent (eleventh rung, HEAD 3c8b3bcabe), the next Lindy-first crux is the one arithmetic piece that verify never needed but SIGNING does: the signature's S = (r + k·a) mod L, a scalar multiply-add modulo the group order. The GREEN scalar rung (crypto/ed25519_scalar.rye) authored reduce and is_canonical — reading and folding — but not COMBINING two scalars by multiplication. mul_add is that last arithmetic piece, and it is non-gated: it is arithmetic on 32-byte integers, holds no key, signs nothing.
think Chose to grow a new file (crypto/ed25519_muladd.rye) beside the frozen GREEN scalar rung rather than edit the proven artifact — one keystone per round, each GREEN file left untouched, the increment sent on its own. The rung composes the GREEN reduce rather than authoring a second reduction.
think The width argument that makes it clean: two 32-byte scalars are each below 2^256, so a·b < 2^512, and a third addend below 2^256 keeps a·b + c below 2^512 − 2^256 < 2^512 (max (2^256−1)² + (2^256−1) = 2^512 − 2^256). So the whole sum fits EXACTLY in 64 little-endian bytes with no carry past the top — asserted, not hoped — and that 64-byte value goes straight into the GREEN reduce. The only new arithmetic is the 256×256→512 schoolbook: 32·32 byte-products into 64 bounded column sums, the addend folded into the low columns, one carry pass into bytes.
think Parity target is Zig's own std.crypto.ecc.Edwards25519.scalar.mulAdd, which computes Scalar.fromBytes(a).mul(b).add(c).toBytes() by Montgomery arithmetic. The result (a·b + c) mod L is independent of the multiply's internal representation, so my full-precision-then-reduce must agree byte-for-byte — proven at selftest, not assumed.
obs GREEN on the first metal pass. Algebraic known-answers hold (1·1+0 = 1, 0·x+c = c mod L, a·1+0 = a mod L), and mul_add equals Zig's scalar.mulAdd byte-for-byte across a spread of 64 scalar triples naming both below-L and above-L operands, plus the all-ones extreme that maximally exercises the 512-bit carry.
obs tame_style bans clean; the new file lands in none of the fail lists. The primitive calls no std.crypto; the only reference to Zig's scalar field is inside the selftest as the independent parity target, exactly as every sibling crypto rung carries it. Honest scope held: purely local arithmetic, no key, no signing, no network, no funds, no real device — no custody gate crossed. When signing eventually calls mul_add, the secret scalar makes a constant-time multiply-add the Season's named timing horizon.
loom witness=crypto_ed25519_muladd_witness algebraic_kats=3 parity_triples=64 all_ones=1 result=GREEN
file crypto/ed25519_muladd.rye scalar multiply-add (a·b + c) mod L in pure Rye, full 512-bit product over the GREEN reduce, parity-GREEN vs Zig scalar.mulAdd
file tools/crypto_ed25519_muladd_witness.rish the witness that builds, runs selftest, asserts the GREEN line
recommend keep-going Every arithmetic piece Ed25519 SIGNING needs now stands GREEN — decode, the scalar ladder, the group law, SHA-512, reduce, and now mul_add. The next Lindy-first crux is the signing composition itself (crypto/ed25519_sign.rye): derive a = clamp(SHA-512(seed)[0..32]) and prefix, A = [a]B, r = SHA-512(prefix ‖ M) mod L, R = [r]B, k = SHA-512(R ‖ A ‖ M) mod L, S = mul_add(k, a, r) — witnessed against Zig-generated keypairs and RFC 8032 §7.1 secret-key vectors, which is NON-gated (a test seed is not a key). Signing with the maintainer's own identity key stays the custody gate. With signing GREEN the Ed25519 scheme is whole and the Lotus signed carry becomes buildable.
